From: Swift Linux <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not run the target to read the wtmp path

meson.build compiles a program that prints WTMPX_FILENAME and runs it to
recover the value:

    result = cc.run(code, name : 'value of WTMPX_FILENAME')
    path_wtmp = result.stdout().strip()

Cross compiling, that program is for the target and the build machine
cannot execute it, so meson stops before configuring finishes:

    meson.build:89:14: ERROR: Can not run test applications in this
    cross environment.

meson has no cross-file property to pre-answer a cc.run() - it raises as
soon as it finds no exe_wrapper - so the value has to come from
somewhere else. It is a compile-time constant in the C library's own
headers, which are right there in the sysroot:

    utmpx.h:      # define WTMPX_FILENAME _PATH_WTMPX
    bits/utmpx.h: # define _PATH_WTMPX    _PATH_WTMP
    paths.h:      # define _PATH_WTMP     "/var/log/wtmp"

so the answer is /var/log/wtmp, and the same for _PATH_WTMPX in the
branch below. Both branches are guarded by a has_header_symbol() check
that only a glibc-style C library passes, and every one of those spells
the path this way; a library that does not define these symbols falls
through to the third branch, which is untouched.

path_wtmp only reaches data/meson.build, where it is substituted into
the D-Bus service file, and PATH_WTMP is still set to the symbol name
rather than the value, so the compiled code keeps reading it from the
header as before.

--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -86,8 +86,9 @@
     return 0;
   }
   '''
-  result = cc.run(code, name : 'value of WTMPX_FILENAME')
-  path_wtmp = result.stdout().strip()
+  # Cross builds cannot run this; WTMPX_FILENAME resolves to _PATH_WTMP,
+  # which every C library defining it spells "/var/log/wtmp".
+  path_wtmp = '/var/log/wtmp'
 
   config_h.set('PATH_WTMP', 'WTMPX_FILENAME')
 elif cc.has_header_symbol('paths.h', '_PATH_WTMPX')
@@ -98,8 +99,8 @@
     return 0;
   }
   '''
-  result = cc.run(code, name : 'value of _PATH_WTMPX')
-  path_wtmp = result.stdout().strip()
+  # Same as above.
+  path_wtmp = '/var/log/wtmp'
 
   config_h.set('PATH_WTMP', '_PATH_WTMPX')
 else
